WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
Let’s talk about everyone’s big hope.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.680
A coronavirus vaccine for all and treatments for the sick.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.920
Hundreds of vaccines and drugs

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.040
are being tested around the world in record time.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.680
What we're trying to do is to take something

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.560
that usually takes 15 years

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.440
and truncate it down to a few months.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.000
We all have questions.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.760
And some of you sent us yours.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.960
When can we expect a vaccine?

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.520
Who will get it first?

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.040
How much will it cost?

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.560
And here’s one from us

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.440
do we need a vaccine to get back to our normal lives?

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.760
There is no cure for SARS-CoV-2

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.120
the coronavirus that causes the disease COVID-19.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.360
But a vaccine and the right drugs

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.120
can help our bodies fight it.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.240
So this is what we’re up against.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.960
The virus has a crown of spikes

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.680
made of protein molecules.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.720
They attach to human cells and act like a key in a lock

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.600
that lets the virus in.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.400
Once inside the virus hijacks

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.200
the cell’s reproductive machinery

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.840
to make copies of itself.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.840
A vaccine protects you by mimicking

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.320
a small part of the virus.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.800
We would take a little part of that protein surface

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.640
of the virus killing the virus and we would inject it

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.480
into people to see if they would make antibodies to it.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:25.240
If that real virus came in then the antibody

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.480
would immediately recognise it

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:27.880
and would immediately take care of it.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.800
But there are different ways of making a vaccine.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.920
Some labs are taking the more traditional route.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.560
They’re testing whether introducing

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.320
different parts of the spike protein into humans

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.440
can trigger an immune response.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.920
But this sort of protein-based vaccine typically takes years

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.040
to develop because scientists have to grow the protein

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.360
and test it’s safe.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.120
So other labs are working on a different strategy

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.000
that’s never been done before.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.000
It involves copying a small section

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.560
of the virus’s RNA genetic code

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:00.160
and injecting that into a person.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.960
That RNA fragment then instructs human cells

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.000
to reproduce certain coronavirus proteins

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.680
and hopefully that triggers an immune response.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.560
Using the RNA approach means you don’t have to grow

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.720
the coronavirus protein in a lab

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.880
the body does all the hard work.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:19.120
Hopefully this will take months out of the process

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.360
and will get us a vaccine sooner.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.720
But again we're in uncharted territory here.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.440
Now usually a vaccine takes up to 10 or even 15 years to test

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.360
manufacture and distribute

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.520
if a vaccine candidate even makes it that far.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.280
But we don't have that kind of time.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.400
Labs around the world got to work

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.920
as soon as a Chinese scientist published

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.440
the virus’s genetic sequence in January.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.360
It's a game with high stakes.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.160
Governments, public institutions, philanthropists

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.440
they’re all involved.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:04.800
Private companies in places like the US

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.960
China and Germany — they’re all competing.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.360
But some researchers are also working together

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:11.760
on open platforms.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.520
We haven't seen such an acceleration

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.520
in sharing information and sharing data.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.400
To give you an idea of how fast things are moving

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.920
it took seven months to start human trials

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.520
during the Zika epidemic.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:24.480
That was a record.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.880
For this new coronavirus it took just 63 days.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.240
But testing on people is just one step.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:34.040
The manufacturing, I think, is going to be the real challenge.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:34.760
And we need to make sure

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.320
that we're ramping that up right now

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.960
alongside the clinical trials that are under way.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.120
Based on most estimates we could get a new vaccine

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.200
sometime early next year.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.520
But it’ll take longer for it to get to millions of people.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.600
One of the vaccine makers Johnson &amp; Johnson

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.520
says that won’t happen before late next year.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.560
And there’s going to be a bottleneck.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.280
So who gets it first?

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.480
During the H1N1 swine flu in 2009

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.560
one of the first vaccines was made in Australia.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.720
The government was criticised

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.360
for making sure it went to Australians first.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.560
The concern this time around is that countries

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.560
will want to do the same thing again.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:14.560
And there’s reason for that concern.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:32.240
Right now there is no international body

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.800
to police distribution of a vaccine.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.280
Not even the WHO has that power.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.920
Nor is there a global plan for a co-ordinated rollout.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.800
Groups like the GAVI vaccine alliance and CEPI, though

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.720
are working to get vaccines to developing countries.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.960
And the WHO has emergency rules in place

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.480
that were set up during the Ebola epidemic.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:55.560
They say any new vaccine should first go to the people

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.240
who need it most — like frontline workers.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.880
There’s also pressure on the pharmaceutical industry

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:12.600
not to chase big profits.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.600
Millions of people need the vaccine

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.400
so the demand will be huge.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.480
And governments can’t afford to pay top dollar.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:22.600
The thing is vaccines are expensive to make.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.600
CEPI and the Wellcome Trust think it’ll cost the industry

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.760
$3 billion to develop, manufacture

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:40.920
and distribute a vaccine globally.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:44.360
It might be worthwhile for a private company

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.680
to make it have a good standing in the public eye

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.960
to say we're going to do this at a reduced cost

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:51.600
this is a humanitarian thing.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.920
The Gates foundation is likely going to blow

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.440
billions of dollars to build production facilities

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.720
for seven vaccine candidates

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:00.640
knowing some of those vaccines

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:01.640
won’t make the grade.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.360
The idea is to ensure we’re ready to go

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:06.000
once a vaccine is found.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.360
We will make it as cheap as we can.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:12.520
And there is no doubt the money will be — should be

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:18.200
found to buy that vaccine for everyone on the planet.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.880
A vaccine is the long-term goal.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.800
So right now scientists are using

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.240
experimental treatments to help people

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:27.560
already sick with COVID-19.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.680
Like plasma therapy which involves collecting antibodies

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.240
from people who’ve recovered

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.920
and injecting them into infected patients.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.440
They’re also trying out drugs in early development

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:40.000
or already on the market for other diseases

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.360
like the antiviral remdesivir that was developed for Ebola

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.000
or hydroxychloroquine used for malaria.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.280
There are many candidates therapeutics or drugs

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:51.320
that are showing some promise or potential.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:53.520
But we need to be exceptionally careful.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.880
It’s extremely important that we rely on the results

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:58.800
that are going to come from trials that will tell us how safe

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.720
and effective these drugs are.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.480
And then there are the so-called treatments

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.000
that have raised alarm bells.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:19.800
The idea of injecting disinfectant

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.400
got lots of laughs online.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.200
I think most people are smart enough to know

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:26.600
that they shouldn't be taking medical

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:28.920
or public health advice from President Trump

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:31.600
they should be listening to expert opinion.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.560
With every vaccine or drug there’s a calculated risk.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:37.160
With measles for instance your chance of dying

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:39.560
from the disease is about one in a thousand.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:41.880
But the chances of having an adverse reaction

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.720
to the vaccine are less than one in a million.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.920
Any medical response is going to be experimental

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:49.760
and might come with side effects.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:52.360
Because no drug or vaccine has been licensed

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:54.040
for this coronavirus.

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:54.880
It’s new.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:57.000
But we also know that vaccines

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.160
have saved millions of lives.

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:11.240
Mumps, measles, the Asian flu

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:14.480
all those epidemics that killed hundreds of millions of people

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:17.040
are nearly all gone now.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:20.960
Experimental vaccines were also used to fight Ebola.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.240
And that epidemic has largely been contained.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.480
The ultimate goal is to slam the brakes

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:28.680
on the virus’s ability to spread.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:30.280
And building immunity is key.

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:33.160
At the moment one infected person gives it to

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:35.360
roughly three other people who each infect

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:37.240
three more and so on.

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:39.880
But if we create what’s called herd immunity

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:41.840
we can bring that right down.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:45.080
The idea is for about 60% of people to become immune

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:47.680
to the coronavirus either by recovering from it

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.400
or getting vaccinated.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:51.960
That way we reduce transmission from one person

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:54.200
to just one other person or less.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:57.920
The key is to get to 60% as fast as possible.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:02.800
That strain of the virus will now no longer be a threat

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:04.160
and it will go away.

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:06.280
But we aren’t there yet.

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:08.640
Which is why the experts say it’ll be a while

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:10.600
before life can return to normal.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:12.560
If we have one-time social distancing

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:15.000
there’s a very good chance that a large number of people

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.040
will remain susceptible to infection.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:18.760
It seems like naturally we're going to enter

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:21.600
into this intermittent social-distancing period.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.200
And in order to reach a high enough level of immunity

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:25.720
in the population our model suggests

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:27.760
that it could take into 2022.

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:30.320
It's also worth remembering that until we get

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:32.880
those treatments and a vaccine most of us

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:34.040
around 80%

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:36.040
can rely on our own immune systems

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:37.440
to fight the coronavirus.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:40.560
So the general advice is to stay healthy, eat well

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:44.120
sleep well and exercise even if you’re stuck at home.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:46.200
Because that’s where we need to be right now

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:48.880
to protect people who are most at risk

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:51.920
while we all wait for the science to catch up.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:56.200
“We don’t know when we’ll be back on

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:58.480
the Start Here set but not to worry.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:00.760
We’re still going to be bringing you more episodes

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:01.960
any way we can.

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:04.320
In the meantime our website aljazeera.com

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:07.280
has all the latest on the coronavirus pandemic.

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:08.080
I’ll see you next week.”

